Duorhythm: EEG hyperscanning during dyadic joint rhythmic action
================================================================

Overview
--------
EEG hyperscanning dataset of pairs (dyads) of participants playing a cooperative
two-player rhythmic shooting game where partners coordinate the timing of
their shots. Resting-state recordings are also included. The data were recorded using Lab Streaming Layer (LSL) and the game was created in Unity.

Ten dyads were recorded. Each session simultaneously recorded both members of a dyad on a single Brain Products amplifier as one 129-channel stream: participant 1 on a 64-channel cap and participant 2 on a second 64-channel cap. In this BIDS release, each dyad is split into two participant ID codes to more easily allow researchers to conduct single-participant analyses. Because both caps share one amplifier clock, the two participants of a pair are sample-aligned. Partner pairs are mapped in participants.tsv (`pair`, `partner`).

Participants and Pairing
--------------------
- 10 dyads -> 20 participants (sub-01 .. sub-20).
- See participants.tsv for the `pair`/`partner` links and self-reported
  `gender`, `age band`, and `acquaintance_length` (how long the pair had known each other).

Tasks
--------------------------
- task-duorhythm: the cooperative shooting game (~20 min). Participants were instructed to shoot a target in three separate conditions: either at alternating times with their partner, simultaneously with their partner, or independently without coordinated timing.
- task-rest: resting-state recordings before (pre) and after (post) the task. Each task-rest was 2 minutes. Participants were instructed to keep their eyes open, hands on the table, and to view the fixation cross on the screen. The two rests use the acq-pre / acq-post label.

Task Instructions (stimuli/)
--------------------------
Participants received instructions for task-duorhythm in a virtual tutorial. To view a sample of instructions provided in the tutorial, please see the .png file in the stimuli/ folder.

Task Structure and Conditions
-----------------------------
The game is organized into 10 blocks x 9 trials = 90 trials of 12 s each. Each
block contains 3 trials of each of three target types. (So, 30 trials per condition
overall.) On each trial, both participants shoot a target together; the target
type indicates how their shots must be timed:

Trial types are: "alternating" (code = 1; target colour = black; behaviour = partners shoot alternately), "simultaneous" (code = 2; target colour = green; behaviour = partners shoot simultaneously), and "free" (code = 3; target colour = blue; behaviour = partners shoot freely with no timing constraint). In each condition, there was also an in-game white "bait" circle that reduces the score when hit and is not a trial type.

Events
------
sub-*_task-duorhythm_events.tsv contains, per participant:
- 90 trial rows with columns `onset`, `duration` (~12 s), `trial_type` (above), `value` (trigger
  code marking the start of the trial type: 11 = alternating, 21 = simultaneous, and 31 = free), `shot_target`, `trial_number`, and `sample`.
- button-press rows (`trial_type` = "buttonpress"): Every shot fired by that
  participant, with `shot_target` indicating the target hit (0 = miss/no target, 1= black, 2 = green, 3 = blue, and 4 = white) and the `trial_number` it falls in. Column definitions are in task-duorhythm_events.json.

Behavioural Responses (beh/)
---------------------------
The continuous game signals (100 Hz) are stored per participant in the behavioural
(beh/) datatype in sub-*_task-duorhythm_recording-gamedata_physio.tsv.gz.
- SamplingFrequency = 100 Hz.
- StartTime aligns the sample grid to the EEG via the shared LSL clock.
- Columns: `crosshair_x`,`crosshair_y`, `crosshair_vx` , `crosshair_vy`, `shot_confirm`,
`shot_target`, and `score`. Descriptions of the columns can be found in the .json files in the beh/ folder.

EEG Acquisition
---------------
Information regarding EEG acquisition can be found in sub-*_task-duorhythm_eeg.json files. Some details are as follows:
- Amplifier: Brain Products BrainAmp.
- Cap: Easycap Standard 64Ch actiCAP snap (CACS-64). 64 channels in the 10/10 layout
  (EEGPlacementScheme).
- No electrode-position files (electrodes.tsv / coordsystem.json) are included, because
  electrode positions were not digitized for individual participants. BrainVision files
  store channel labels only, not 3D coordinates. To obtain template coordinates, apply MNE's
  `easycap-M1` montage.
- Sampling frequency: 1000 Hz. Data stored in microvolts (BrainVision float).
- Reference electrode: FCz. Ground electrode: Fpz. Impedance kept below 25 kilohm.
- The online low- and high-cutoff frequencies: 0.016 - 1000 Hz (respectively). Power line noise at 60 Hz. SoftwareFilters = n/a.

Questionnaire (phenotype/)
--------------------------
The self-report questionnaire has five parts. Part 1 (demographics + relationship) is in participants.tsv. Parts 2-5 are four distinct 9-item Likert subscales (1 = Strongly Disagree to 7 =
Strongly Agree), each with its own phenotype file (one row per participant; items are
positional item_01 to item_09 with the exact wording in the matching .json):
- phenotype/display_comfort.tsv (Part 2, pre-task)
- phenotype/game_familiarity.tsv (Part 3, pre-task)
- phenotype/coordination_skill.tsv (Part 4, post-task)
- phenotype/coordination_level.tsv (Part 5, post-task)

Data-Quality Notes
-------------------------------------------
- sub-02, sub-04, sub-10, sub-20: only the pre-task half of the
  questionnaire was recorded (due to software logging limitation + procedural oversight).
  The pre-task questionnaire (display_comfort = Part 2, game_familiarity = Part 3)
  are retained, and the post-experiment questionnaire (coordination_skill = Part 4,
  coordination_level = Part 5) are n/a (see KnownIssues in those phenotype JSONs).

Conversion
-----------------------
Converted from the original Lab Streaming Layer .xdf recordings with
the pipeline notebooks (`1_eeg_to_bids.ipynb` for EEG, `2_build_events.ipynb` for events, `3_build_physio.ipynb` for continuous game data, and `4_build_phenotype.ipynb` for the questionnaire data, which can be found at [https://github.com/Froese-Unit/duorhythm-code](https://github.com/Froese-Unit/duorhythm-code).

Ethics: approved by the OIST Human Subjects Research Review Committee, approval
no. HSR-2024-019-5.

